---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site, contactEmail } from '../lib/site.js';
import { meta, probes } from '../lib/data.js';
---

<Base
  title="Contact and corrections"
  description="How to report an error, challenge a measurement, or run a probe."
  current="/contact/"
>
  <h1>Contact and corrections</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    This site publishes measurements and legal summaries that affect real
    architecture decisions. If something here is wrong, the fastest way to fix
    it is to tell us, and every correction route below is a public one.
  </p>

  <h2>Report an error</h2>
  <p>
    <a href={`${site.repo}/issues/new`}>Open an issue</a> on GitHub. That is
    the preferred channel for everything: a measurement that looks wrong, a
    residency entry that misreads the law, a broken endpoint, a region we have
    not added yet.
  </p>
  <p class="small muted">
    Public and threaded, so a correction and its evidence stay attached to the
    claim they fixed. Private email cannot do that.
  </p>

  {contactEmail ? (
    <>
      <h2>Private contact</h2>
      <p>
        For anything that should not start in public — a legal correction, a
        security report, a network operator asking about our probing — write to{' '}
        <a href={`mailto:${contactEmail}`}>{contactEmail}</a>.
      </p>
    </>
  ) : (
    <>
      <h2>Private contact</h2>
      <p class="muted">
        No private address is published in this build. Use GitHub issues, or
        set <code>PUBLIC_CONTACT_EMAIL</code> at build time to publish one.
      </p>
    </>
  )}

  <h2>If you operate a network we measure</h2>
  <p>
    We send a small number of ICMP echo requests and TLS handshakes on a fixed
    schedule, to published endpoints, from a published list of probes. The full
    method is in the <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a>, including every
    endpoint we touch.
  </p>
  <p>
    If our traffic is a problem for you, open an issue or write to us and we
    will stop measuring your network. We are measuring the internet, not
    testing anyone's patience.
  </p>

  <h2>Run a probe</h2>
  <p>
    The measurements are only as good as the number of vantage points. If you
    have a connection in the Gulf — home, office, a small VPS — you can
    contribute one. The agent is a single binary with no dependencies, it needs
    no root, and it measures outbound only: it opens no ports and reads nothing
    about your network beyond the route to a handful of cloud endpoints.
  </p>
  <p><a href={site.repo}>Source and setup instructions</a></p>
  <p class="small muted">
    {probes.length === 1
      ? 'There is currently one probe. Every additional one makes every figure on this site more defensible.'
      : `There are currently ${probes.length} probes.`}
  </p>

  <h2>What this is not</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Not legal advice.</strong> The residency pages summarise
      legislation for engineers choosing a region. They are not a substitute
      for a qualified practitioner in the relevant jurisdiction, and several
      entries are explicitly marked draft.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Not a cloud provider ranking.</strong> Latency from one set of
      vantage points is one input among many.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Not affiliated</strong> with any cloud provider, telecom operator
      or regulator.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who maintains this</h2>
  <p>
    {site.maintainer}, from Muscat. The project is open source; the
    measurement code, the residency entries and the rules behind the advisor
    are all in <a href={site.repo}>the repository</a>, and so is the history of
    every change to them.
  </p>

  {meta.generated_at && (
    <p class="small muted">
      The data on this site was exported on {new Date(meta.generated_at).toISOString().slice(0, 16).replace('T', ' ')} UTC.
    </p>
  )}
</Base>
